.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt SYNC 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sync ,
.Nm syncfs ,
.Nm fsync ,
.Nm fdatasync
.Nd flush buffered file system writes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft void
.Fn sync "void"
.Ft int
.Fn syncfs "int fd"
.Ft int
.Fn fsync "int fd"
.Ft int
.Fn fdatasync "int fd"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn sync
flushes buffered file system writes to stable storage.
For each active mount with a device that is still present and admits an
operation, it runs the file system's sync operation, writes the device's
dirty buffers, and, if those writes succeed, flushes the device.
It then writes every dirty page of the disk-backed KRFFS window.
The page-cache flush does nothing when KRFFS is a direct RAM module.
.Pp
.Fn sync
ignores write-back errors.
Buffers whose writes fail remain dirty for a later attempt.
A failed buffer write makes mounts on that device read-only for the
rest of their lifetime.
.Pp
.Fn syncfs
flushes everything
.Fn sync
does, and ignores write-back errors the same way, where Linux flushes
only the file system
.Fa fd
is on and reports a write-back error.
It accepts any open descriptor but one opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.Pp
.Fn fsync
accepts any open descriptor
.Fa fd
but one opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
which returns
.Er EBADF
before anything is flushed, as on Linux; it does not check the
descriptor's type.
It first writes resident shared-mapping pages of the file.
A failed write at the last unmap keeps its pages and file open for retry;
the next fsync reports that error once even if its retry succeeds.
A pending mapping release is completed before fsync chooses its writer.
For a descriptor on a mounted file system, it runs that file system's
fsync operation, or writes the device's dirty buffers if the file
system has no such operation.
On success it also flushes the device.
For a KRFFS boot-root file, or a descriptor without a mount, it flushes
the entire page cache.
That flush does nothing on a direct RAM root.
An in-flight page write keeps its slot and frame reserved.
A competing page-cache flush skips that page and reports an error for retry.
Failed writes and stores made during a write remain dirty for a later flush.
On a mounted KRFFS volume it writes only the named file: the blocks its
writes and truncations left dirty, which follow it when it moves, and
its header, which holds its length, mode, owner and times.
On FAT32 and exFAT it writes the file's dirty blocks and then the
dirty blocks no one file owns.
On ext2 it writes, for a regular file, the bitmaps and descriptors
dirtied for the file, its data and pointer blocks, and its inode's
table block, which holds other inodes too; for any other file, the
device's dirty buffers.
A mounted KRFFS volume made read-only after a failed write writes every
pending buffer of the device instead.
A failed retry is returned to the caller; the device is flushed only
after writeback succeeds.
.Pp
On ext2, fsync first drains all pending inodes on the mount in dependency order.
The first delayed write or flush error remains observable by every later
fsync until unmount, including after close, reopen and successful retry.
A retry can finish an unmount, but the volume remains marked invalid.
.Pp
.Fn fdatasync
runs the file system's fdatasync operation where it has one, and
otherwise does what
.Fn fsync
does.
Only ext2 has one: it leaves out the inode's table block when only the
inode's times have changed since an earlier call wrote the block, and
writes it after a change of the size, block map, mode, owner or link
count.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 36 for
.Fn sync ,
344 for
.Fn syncfs ,
118 for
.Fn fsync ,
and 148 for
.Fn fdatasync .
The descriptor is passed in EBX.
On x86-64 the numbers are 162, 306, 74 and 75, and the descriptor is
passed in RDI.
.Pp
.Nm sync_file_range
is i386's 314, which takes the descriptor, the offset and the length in
two words each, low word first, and the flags; the x86-64 kernel runs the
same call for a program that enters it by
.Ql int $0x80 ,
which
.Xr ia32 7
describes.
Its native x86-64 call is 277: the descriptor in RDI, the offset in RSI,
the length in RDX and the flags in R10.
As on Linux, a descriptor not open is
.Er EBADF
whatever the other arguments, then an unknown flag, a negative offset or
length or a range past the largest offset is
.Er EINVAL ,
and an object other than a regular file, directory, block device or
symbolic link
.Er ESPIPE .
With
.Dv SYNC_FILE_RANGE_WRITE
it writes out the whole file's data, as
.Fn fdatasync
does, which covers the range.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn sync
has no return value; its kernel call always returns 0.
.Fn syncfs
returns 0, or -1 with
.Va errno
set when the descriptor is not one it accepts.
.Fn fsync
and
.Fn fdatasync
return 0 on success, or -1 on failure with
.Va errno
set.
The kernel returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/vfsprobe.c
opens a file read-only, calls
.Fn fsync
on its descriptor, and requires success.
.Sh ERRORS
.Fn syncfs ,
.Fn fsync
and
.Fn fdatasync
can fail with:
.Bl -tag -width "[ESRCH]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EIO
For
.Fn fsync
and
.Fn fdatasync ,
a write-back or device flush failed, or the mounted device was removed,
or a KRFFS boot-root page write was already in flight.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
